      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
